From c6375118dd58b548884b8dafc70596e8c3362d1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:37:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 431/433] macro_rules: a `stmt` fragment can be an item
 (`extern crate askama;`)

askama_macros 0.16.0 (zoxide 0.10.0) src/lib.rs:6: "BUG:
src/macro_rules/eval.cpp:2443: TODO: Macro_InvokeRules_MatchPattern - No arm
matched - (18, 83)".

askama_derive's `make_derive_template! { #[proc_macro_derive(..)] pub fn
derive_template() { extern crate askama; } }` matches the body with
`$($import:stmt)+` and forwards it through `quote!($($import)*)`. A `stmt`
fragment covers item statements (rustc: items, `let`, expressions), but:

- the arm matcher's lookahead (consume_stmt) knew `let` and expressions only,
  so the arm failed at `extern` (18 tokens in);
- the capture parsed with Parse_Stmt into an expression node, which cannot
  hold an item;
- `stringify!` of an item fragment was the placeholder `/*:item*/`, and
  quote!'s `$other:tt` arm feeds every forwarded fragment through
  `parse(stringify!($other))`;
- an item fragment given to a proc macro directly was a TODO in
  expand/proc_macro.cpp.

Now a statement that starts an item (the block parser's rule, after any outer
attributes: item keywords, `pub`, `crate` not followed by `::`, `const` /
`unsafe` not followed by `{`, `async fn`, `union Ident`, `auto trait`) is
matched by consume_item and captured as an `$i:item` fragment, which a block
already takes back as one of its items (Parse_ExprBlockLine_WithItems).
Token::to_str prints an item fragment as source (a new DumpAST_Item over the
module printer, so `extern crate askama;` comes out as the `extern crate
"askama" as askama;` the parser also reads), and the proc-macro serialiser
sends it as an invisible group of the item's tokens (visit_named_item).
Attributes before a non-item statement go onto the statement node.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.1
